  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 713 PM MDT Sat Oct 17 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nUpper level ridging over the Western CONUS early this morning will \ncontinue to be flattened as a potent shortwave over the Gulf of \nAlaska slides east over British Columbia and the Canadian Prairie \nProvinces through the day today. Increasing cross barrier flow over \nthe Northern Rockies will lead to strengthening southwest to west \nwinds across Southwest through North Central Montana, most notably \nalong the Rocky Mountain Front and International Border. These \ndownsloping southwest to west winds will lead to another day of \nabove normal temperatures across the region, with highs peaking in \nthe mid-60s to near 70 degrees in the valleys of Southwest and \nCentral Montana to the low 70s to near 80 degrees over the plains of \nCentral and North Central Montana.  \"Cooler\" temperatures are then \nexpected into the day on Sunday in wake of a weak Pacific front, \nwhich will slide east and over the Northern Rockies late tonight, \nwith highs topping out in the upper 50s to low 60s. Despite \npersistent zonal flow over the Northern Rockies on Sunday weak \nsurface high pressure settling in over the Pacific Northwest and \nNorthern Rockies will help to support light surfaces winds and \ncontinued dry conditions. - Moldan \n\nThrough next week split flow will set up with an upper-level low \ncentered off the coast of California. This setup will put Montana\nunder southwesterly flow with moisture being funnel into \nSouthwest Montana for daily precipitation chances. The forecast\nuncertainty increases significantly towards late next week with \nsome hints that an upper-level trough could move south and merge \nwith the low as it moves off to the northeast. This could bring\nmore widespread chances for precipitation and some cooler\ntemperatures although there is still a bit of disagreement on what\nexactly this means for our area.  -thor\n\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nGusty to strong winds will continue across Central and North-\nCentral Montana through the rest of the afternoon and evening\nhours. The strongest winds will be more confined to the Rocky\nMountain Front where sustained winds of 40 to 50 mph and gusts up\nto 85 mph are possible.\n\nWhile frequent gusts in the 40s are occurring across the Northern\nPlains and a gust up to 50 mph has been observed in Great Falls,\nthere is still low confidence in wind speeds/gusts reaching full\nhigh wind criteria. As a result, no expansion was made to the High\nWind Warning with this forecast.\n\nWinds will start to taper off towards sunset into the overnight\nhours. Periods of gusty winds may still continue for a little\nwhile but generally the worst of the winds are expected to be done\nby 8 PM.  -thor",
